        "heading": "What are the side effects of Mazdutide?",
        "text": "Mazdutide's side-effect profile is dominated by gastrointestinal effects, consistent with the GLP-1 class, and these are most pronounced during dose escalation. The glucagon-receptor arm introduces mechanism-specific considerations, including modest heart-rate increases and the counter-regulatory glucose effect that the GLP-1 arm is designed to offset. Common (most users) Nausea. The most common effect, especially while escalating the dose. Diarrhea and vomiting. Common at higher doses and during titration. Decreased appetite. An expected pharmacologic effect that can be excessive in some individuals. Constipation. Reported alongside the other GI effects. Injection-site reactions. Mild redness or irritation at the injection site. Less common (moderate) Increased heart rate. Modest heart-rate increases are seen across the class and are relevant to the glucagon arm. Headache and fatigue. Typically mild, most noticeable in the days after a dose increase. Blood-glucose effects. Glucagon raises blood glucose in isolation; mazdutide's GLP-1 arm offsets this, and the diabetes indication reflects net glycemic benefit. Glucose is monitored. Serious (rare — call a doctor) Pancreatitis. A class-level concern for GLP-1-based agents. Symptoms: severe abdominal pain radiating to the back, persistent vomiting. Gallbladder problems. Rapid weight loss raises gallstone risk across the class. Symptoms: severe upper-right abdominal pain, jaundice, fever. Severe allergic reactions. Rare. Symptoms: difficulty breathing, facial or throat swelling, hives. Seek emergency care. In the GLORY trials, most adverse events were gastrointestinal, mild-to-moderate, and concentrated during escalation; slow titration is the primary mitigation. The published safety data is drawn from Chinese cohorts over 48–60 weeks."
