        "text": "Back to Peptide Library Melanotan II Skin Health &amp; Anti-Aging Research-Grade Last reviewed: May 24, 2026 Quick Facts What it is A lab-made melanocortin receptor agonist studied for skin tanning and pigmentation and for libido &mdash; non-selective across MC1R&ndash;MC5R, which drives both its effects and a broad side-effect burden. How it&apos;s taken Subcutaneous injection, starting from a 0.1 mg test dose Half-life ~hours in plasma; pigmentation effects accumulate over weeks Typical research dose 0.25&ndash;1 mg per dose; never exceed 1 mg per dose Research status Not FDA-approved &mdash; research use only; health agency warnings in the US, UK, Australia, and EU. [[REDACTED]] What is Melanotan II? Melanotan II (also known as MT-II or MT2) is a synthetic cyclic heptapeptide &mdash; a melanocyte-stimulating hormone analog &mdash; that acts as a non-selective agonist of the melanocortin receptors (MC1R, MC3R, MC4R, MC5R). It was developed in the 1980s at the University of Arizona as a successor to Melanotan I (afamelanotide, FDA-approved as Scenesse for erythropoietic protoporphyria). Originally developed as a sunless tanning agent, MT-II also produces strong sexual arousal effects (via MC4R) and appetite suppression. It is NOT FDA-approved for any indication, and health agencies in multiple countries have warned against its use. PT-141 (bremelanotide / Vyleesi) was specifically derived from MT-II by removing the C-terminal amide group &mdash; eliminating the strong tanning effect while preserving sexual arousal effects. MT-II is associated with documented serious adverse events including rhabdomyolysis, renal injury, and reversible darkening of moles. New to peptide research? Start with the basics Reported benefits: Sunless tanning (skin darkening via MC1R-mediated melanin production) Appetite suppression (MC4R activation) Sexual arousal / erection support in males (MC3R/MC4R) Reduced UV exposure required for tan development Reported risks (genuinely worth flagging upfront): Mole darkening, new moles, reported melanoma cases (causation unclear), rhabdomyolysis, renal infarction, sympathomimetic toxicity, severe nausea, priapism. See the full Side Effects &amp; Safety section. Common research dose: Starting dose: 0.1 mg subcutaneously to assess tolerance. Typical research range: 0.25–1 mg per dose, 2–3 times per week during loading phase, then weekly maintenance. NEVER exceed 1 mg per dose in research contexts. Where to buy: PP maintains a vetted list of peptide vendors with verified discount codes. See Verified Discount Codes for current options. [[REDACTED]] [[REDACTED]] Related Peptides PT-141 GHK-Cu KPV Need to calculate a dose? Use the Prof. Peptide dosage calculator for accurate reconstitution and dosing math. Open Calculator"
