        "heading": "Page content",
        "text": "Back to Peptide Library NAD+ Longevity Research-Grade Last reviewed: May 24, 2026 Quick Facts What it is Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide &mdash; a coenzyme in every living cell, central to energy metabolism, DNA repair, and sirtuin activity. A small-molecule cofactor (not a peptide), studied for longevity, energy, and cellular repair. How it&apos;s taken Subcutaneous injection or IV infusion (clinical); oral precursors (NMN, NR) also used Half-life Short plasma half-life; cellular NAD+ pools persist via downstream conversion Typical research dose SC 50&ndash;100 mg once or twice weekly; IV 250&ndash;1000 mg; oral precursors (NMN/NR) 250&ndash;1000 mg daily Research status Not FDA-approved as injectable therapy; oral precursors (NMN/NR) sold as supplements. Not WADA-prohibited. [[REDACTED]] What is NAD+? NAD+ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide, also written NADH in its reduced form and historically called Coenzyme 1) is a coenzyme present in every cell of every living organism. It plays a central role in cellular metabolism, energy production, DNA repair, and gene expression regulation. Unlike most peptides on Prof. Peptide, NAD+ is technically a small-molecule cofactor rather than a peptide &mdash; but it&apos;s included because it&apos;s commonly stacked with peptides in longevity protocols and sold by the same vendors. NAD+ levels decline ~50% from age 20 to 60, and this decline is implicated in many hallmarks of aging including mitochondrial dysfunction, accumulated DNA damage, and reduced sirtuin activity. NAD+ supplementation has emerged as one of the most researched longevity interventions of the past decade. Often paired with Epitalon for combined cellular metabolism + telomere maintenance. New to peptide research? Start with the basics Reported benefits: Restoration of cellular NAD+ levels to youthful range Sirtuin enzyme activation (SIRT1, SIRT3, SIRT6) &mdash; longevity gene activation Enhanced mitochondrial function and ATP production Improved DNA repair via PARP activation Cognitive support and neuroprotection Combined energy + recovery + longevity effects Common research dose: Subcutaneous injection: 50–100 mg once or twice weekly. IV infusion (clinical settings): 250–1000 mg over 2–4 hours. Oral precursors (NMN, NR): 250–1000 mg daily. Sublingual NAD+: 50–125 mg daily. Where to buy: PP maintains a vetted list of peptide vendors with verified discount codes. See Verified Discount Codes for current options. [[REDACTED]] [[REDACTED]] Related Peptides Epitalon GHK-Cu MOTS-c Thymosin Alpha-1 Related Blends NAD+ + MOTS-c + 5-Amino-1MQ Comparisons Epitalon vs NAD+ Need to calculate a dose? Use the Prof. Peptide dosage calculator for accurate reconstitution and dosing math. Open Calculator"
