        "text": "The PT-141 + Oxytocin blend has no clinically validated combination protocol. Research-community dosing layers each peptide&apos;s individual established protocol. PT-141 is dosed as a subcutaneous injection from a reconstituted lyophilized vial; oxytocin is typically administered as an intranasal spray or sublingual troche rather than by injection. PT-141 (Bremelanotide) &mdash; SC injection protocol: Research dose range. 1&ndash;2 mg subcutaneously per use. The FDA-approved Vyleesi dose is 1.75 mg; research-grade use typically starts at 1 mg to assess nausea tolerance and titrates up if needed. Timing. Inject approximately 45 minutes before planned sexual activity. Onset is slower than PDE5 inhibitors &mdash; the 45-minute minimum lead time should not be compressed. Frequency limits. No more than once per 24-hour period; no more than approximately 8 times per month (aligned with Vyleesi prescribing guidance). Frequency limits are particularly important for avoiding focal hyperpigmentation with repeated injection-site use. Site. Abdomen or upper thigh. Rotate sites to reduce hyperpigmentation risk at any one location. Oxytocin &mdash; intranasal spray or sublingual troche: Research dose range. 20&ndash;40 IU intranasal or sublingual, approximately 20&ndash;30 minutes before activity. Published human social-bonding and sexual-function studies have used 24 IU intranasal as the most common dose. Intranasal administration. One spray (typically 4 IU per actuation) per nostril, repeating to reach total dose. Allow 30 seconds between actuations. Avoid blowing the nose immediately after. Sublingual troche. Allow to dissolve completely under the tongue. Avoid eating or drinking during dissolution. Source note. Oxytocin is more commonly sourced through compounding pharmacies as a nasal spray or troche than through research peptide vendors. Availability from peptide vendors varies; verify with the specific vendor before ordering. PT-141 reconstitution &mdash; research-grade vials Typical SKUs. PT-141 research vials are commonly sold as 5 mg or 10 mg lyophilized powder. Reconstitute with bacteriostatic water (BAC water). Swirl gently &mdash; do not shake. Reconstitution math (5 mg vial). Adding 2.5 mL BAC water to a 5 mg vial yields 2 mg/mL. A 1 mg dose requires 0.5 mL (50 units on a U-100 syringe); a 1.75 mg dose requires 0.875 mL (~88 units). Adding 5 mL yields 1 mg/mL &mdash; 1 mg per 1 mL (100 units), which is easier to measure precisely. Need to calculate your dose? Use the dosage calculator . This blend is not FDA-approved as a combination. Dosing guidance is derived from each component&apos;s individual research literature. There are no approved retail dosing standards for the combination."
