        "heading": "Page content",
        "text": "Back to Peptide Library Thymosin Alpha-1 Gut Health &amp; Immunity Approved Internationally Last reviewed: May 24, 2026 Quick Facts What it is A 28-amino-acid immune-modulating peptide from the thymus &mdash; it promotes T-cell maturation and is studied for immune support, chronic viral infection, and as a vaccine adjuvant. How it&apos;s taken Subcutaneous injection, twice weekly to daily depending on protocol Half-life ~2 hours plasma &mdash; immune effects on T-cell maturation persist far longer Typical research dose 1.6 mg twice weekly (Zadaxin standard), or 250&ndash;750 mcg daily for research immune support Research status Not FDA-approved in the US; approved as Zadaxin in 35+ countries &mdash; US research use only. [[REDACTED]] What is Thymosin Alpha-1? Thymosin Alpha-1 (also written T&alpha;1 or TA-1) is a 28-amino-acid peptide naturally produced by the thymus gland, where it plays a critical role in T-cell maturation and immune system development. Synthetic Thymosin Alpha-1 (thymalfasin, brand name Zadaxin) is approved as a prescription drug in over 35 countries for treating chronic hepatitis B and hepatitis C, often as an adjunct to interferon therapy. It is also used as an immune-supportive treatment in cancer patients undergoing chemotherapy. While NOT FDA-approved in the United States, Thymosin Alpha-1 has decades of clinical use abroad and one of the strongest evidence bases of any peptide on Prof. Peptide. Distinct from Thymosin Beta-4 (TB-500) despite the shared name. Often paired with BPC-157 or KPV for combined immune + tissue / anti-inflammatory protocols. New to peptide research? Start with the basics Reported benefits: Enhanced T-cell maturation and function Improved immune response to chronic viral infections (Hepatitis B/C, CMV) Adjunctive support during cancer chemotherapy Modulation of autoimmune conditions (without immunosuppression) Restoration of immune function in immunocompromised states Approved internationally for clinical use (35+ countries) Common research dose: Subcutaneous injection: 1.6 mg twice weekly (standard Zadaxin protocol for hepatitis), or 1.6 mg daily for 5 days followed by 2 days off (cancer adjunct protocol). Research peptide protocols often use 250–750 mcg daily. Cycle length: 4–12 weeks. Where to buy: PP maintains a vetted list of peptide vendors with verified discount codes. See Verified Discount Codes for current options. [[REDACTED]] [[REDACTED]] Related Peptides KPV BPC-157 TB-500 GHK-Cu NAD+ Need to calculate a dose? Use the Prof. Peptide dosage calculator for accurate reconstitution and dosing math. Open Calculator"
