        "heading": "Page content",
        "text": "Back to Supplement Library Bacopa Monnieri Cognitive &amp; Nootropic Last reviewed: May 26, 2026 Also Known As: Brahmi, water hyssop, thyme-leaved gratiola, herb of grace, BM, bacosides Supplement Class: Ayurvedic adaptogenic herb / cumulative nootropic (bacoside-standardized triterpenoid saponin extract) Evidence Tier: Strong — Kongkeaw 2014 meta-analysis of 9 RCTs; multiple independent 12-week RCTs (Stough 2001, Roodenrys 2002, Calabrese 2008, Stough 2008) consistently demonstrating chronic memory and anxiolytic effects; among the better-supported herbal nootropics in the Western literature What is Bacopa monnieri? Bacopa monnieri is a creeping aquatic herb native to South Asian wetlands and a cornerstone of Ayurvedic medicine, where it has been used for memory and cognitive support for centuries. Its active compounds — bacosides A and B (triterpenoid saponins) — drive the modern nootropic case. Unlike most cognitive supplements that produce acute, same-day effects, Bacopa works through structural neural remodeling: dendritic branching, synaptic protein synthesis, acetylcholinesterase inhibition, and antioxidant neuroprotection. The cognitive benefits are cumulative and build over 8–12 weeks of consistent use. It is one of the better-studied herbal nootropics in Western science — multiple independent 12-week RCTs (Stough, Roodenrys, Calabrese) and the Kongkeaw 2014 meta-analysis consistently show improvements in delayed recall and information retention in healthy adults. It pairs naturally with cognitive peptides that work on a different timescale: peptides handle the acute focus and resilience; Bacopa handles the chronic memory consolidation. Reported benefits: Improved delayed recall and memory consolidation (most-replicated RCT finding) Reduced rate of forgetting newly learned material (Roodenrys 2002) Anxiety and depression reduction in older adults (Calabrese 2008) Speed of information processing and rapid visual information processing (Stough trials) Antioxidant neuroprotection in hippocampal neurons Mild acetylcholinesterase inhibition (same mechanism class as donepezil, at lower magnitude) Serotonergic modulation and cortisol response shaping under chronic stress Common dose: 300 mg once daily of an extract standardized to 50–55% bacosides (the dose used in nearly all positive RCTs). Split dosing (150 mg twice daily with meals) is an option for GI tolerability. Higher doses (600 mg/day) are tolerated but don&apos;t clearly accelerate the 8–12 week timeline. Where to buy: Available over-the-counter from most supplement retailers. Quality varies — particularly around bacoside standardization. Review the quality-markers checklist in the Where to Buy section below before purchasing. [[REDACTED]] Related Supplements Alpha-GPC Lion&apos;s Mane Phosphatidylserine Rhodiola Rosea Caffeine + L-Theanine"
