        "text": "Back to Supplement Library Chromium Metabolic &amp; Weight Loss Last reviewed: May 26, 2026 Also Known As: Chromium picolinate, chromium polynicotinate, GTF chromium, trivalent chromium, Cr(III) Supplement Class: Essential trace mineral / insulin-receptor cofactor (via chromodulin) / glycemic adjunct Evidence Tier: Strong — Anderson 1997 dose-ranging RCT in T2D, Cefalu 2010 hyperinsulinemic-euglycemic clamp confirmation, Suksomboon 2014 meta-analysis (25 RCTs, n=1,690) demonstrating significant HbA1c and fasting glucose reductions in diabetic populations; Cochrane 2013 weight-loss meta-analysis confirms modest but real effect What is chromium? Chromium is an essential trace mineral required in microgram amounts that functions as a cofactor for insulin signaling. It is incorporated into chromodulin (also called glucose tolerance factor / GTF), a small oligopeptide that binds the activated insulin receptor and amplifies its tyrosine kinase activity — improving GLUT4 translocation, glucose uptake into muscle and fat cells, and overall insulin sensitivity. Chromium has one of the deeper RCT bases of any metabolic mineral, with Anderson 1997 and Suksomboon 2014 meta-analysis evidence demonstrating significant HbA1c and fasting glucose reductions in type 2 diabetes populations. The weight-loss evidence (Cochrane 2013) is modest but real. Chromium pairs naturally with GLP-1 receptor agonist peptides like Semaglutide and Tirzepatide because the mechanisms operate on different layers of the same metabolic system — peptides upstream at GLP-1 receptors and gastric emptying, chromium downstream at insulin-receptor signaling. Reported benefits: Reduced HbA1c and fasting glucose in T2D (−0.55% HbA1c per Suksomboon 2014 meta-analysis) Improved insulin sensitivity via chromodulin / insulin-receptor amplification (Cefalu 2010 clamp-confirmed) Modest weight loss in overweight populations (~1 kg per Cochrane 2013) Reduced carbohydrate cravings and food intake (Anton 2008) Improved lipid profile (LDL, total cholesterol, triglycerides) in dyslipidemic populations Correction of dietary chromium gap common in processed-food-heavy diets Synergistic with berberine, metformin, and GLP-1 peptides for comprehensive glycemic support Common dose: 200–400 mcg/day chromium picolinate with a meal for general metabolic baseline; 500–1000 mcg/day (often split AM/PM) for T2D / insulin resistance protocols (the Anderson 1997 high-dose arm). Always with food, ideally with the largest carbohydrate-containing meal. Where to buy: Widely available as low-cost chromium picolinate. Quality bar is moderate — picolinate form and clean mcg disclosure are the main markers. Review the quality-markers checklist in the Where to Buy section below. [[REDACTED]] Related Supplements Berberine Alpha Lipoic Acid Magnesium Vitamin D3 Green Tea Extract"
