        "heading": "Side effects and interactions",
        "text": "Curcumin has an excellent safety profile in clinical trials. The main practical considerations are mild GI effects, the antiplatelet interaction with anticoagulants (clinically relevant for surgery and chronic anticoagulation users), and iron-absorption interference. Common (mostly transient) Mild GI discomfort — occasional nausea, diarrhea, or stomach upset at higher doses (1 g+). Resolves with food coadministration or dose reduction. Yellow-orange staining of urine or stool — harmless cosmetic effect from curcumin pigment excretion. No documented serious adverse events at supplemental doses across the human trial base. Less common (watch-list) Antiplatelet effect (clinically relevant). Mild but documented; matters for users on anticoagulants and for pre-surgical considerations. Stop curcumin 1–2 weeks before scheduled surgery. Iron absorption interference — curcumin chelates iron in the gut and may reduce iron absorption. Relevant for iron-deficient or iron-supplementing users; separate iron supplement dosing by 2+ hours. Gallbladder issues — curcumin stimulates gallbladder contraction; may exacerbate symptoms in users with gallstones or biliary obstruction. Drug and supplement interactions Anticoagulants (warfarin, apixaban, rivaroxaban, dabigatran, clopidogrel). Additive antiplatelet/anticoagulant effect. Coordinate with prescribing clinician; INR monitoring for warfarin users. NSAIDs and aspirin. Additive antiplatelet effect; modest additional bleeding risk. Generally tolerated but worth flagging for chronic high-dose NSAID users. Iron supplements — separate dosing by 2+ hours to avoid chelation-mediated absorption interference. Chemotherapy — curcumin has both pro- and anti-cancer effects depending on context; some chemotherapy regimens may be affected. Coordinate with oncologist. Diabetes medications — curcumin modestly improves insulin sensitivity; additive glucose-lowering with insulin, sulfonylureas, GLP peptides. Monitor glucose during titration. Pregnancy and breastfeeding — high supplemental doses not recommended; culinary use is fine. Insufficient data for therapeutic dosing safety."
