        "text": "Green tea extract has been extensively studied but several practical questions remain — particularly around hepatotoxicity mechanism, individual responder susceptibility, and clinical-outcome translation for some claimed benefits. Hepatotoxicity mechanism and individual susceptibility. Why some users develop liver injury at supplemental EGCG doses while most do not isn&apos;t fully characterized. Genetic factors in EGCG metabolism, baseline liver status, and other susceptibility factors are areas of ongoing research. Long-term cancer prevention effects. Observational studies in high-tea-consuming East Asian populations suggest cancer prevention benefit. Whether supplemental extract produces equivalent effects, or whether the food-matrix benefits of brewed tea are specifically protective, isn&apos;t precisely characterized. Optimal EGCG dose for various indications. Different applications likely have different optimal doses; current trials have used variable doses without precise dose-response characterization. Tolerance development to thermogenic effect. Some evidence suggests thermogenic response to catechin-caffeine combinations diminishes with chronic use. Optimal cycling vs continuous use isn&apos;t precisely characterized. Decaffeinated extract effect size. Loss of catechin-caffeine synergy means decaf extract should have smaller thermogenic effect. Quantitative magnitude isn&apos;t precisely characterized. Cardiovascular outcome trials at scale. Surrogate markers (LDL, BP, antioxidant markers) improve with supplementation; large hard-outcome cardiovascular trials at REDUCE-IT scale don&apos;t exist. Brewed tea vs extract head-to-head for chronic outcomes. Both are used in different populations and contexts; direct head-to-head trials at matched catechin doses are limited."
