        "text": "L-citrulline is widely available as pharmaceutical-grade powder or capsules. Like other amino acid supplements, quality is more uniform than in botanical extracts — the active ingredient is a defined molecule. Cost varies several-fold between brands; powder is more cost-efficient than capsules at supplemental doses. Quality markers to look for Pure L-citrulline (free amino acid) — clean dose for vascular/sexual health use. Read the label for grams of L-citrulline per serving. For pre-workout: L-citrulline malate (2:1 form) — note that 8 g citrulline malate provides approximately 5.3 g pure citrulline + 2.7 g malic acid. See the citrulline malate page for the ergogenic discussion. cGMP-certified manufacturing facility — minimum bar. Third-party tested for purity — USP, NSF, or ConsumerLab certifications. Heavy metals and microbial testing. Powder for therapeutic doses — 3–6 g/day is cheaper from powder than from capsules. Mixes well in water or juice. Reputable brands — Now Foods, Thorne, Pure Encapsulations, Bulk Supplements, NutraBio, Nutricost. Brand premium is mostly QC transparency. Watermelon extract is acceptable but variable — standardized to citrulline content if listed. Cost-inefficient at supplemental doses vs pure L-citrulline. Avoid &quot;NO booster&quot; proprietary blends — these usually combine small doses of citrulline, arginine, beet powder, and stim ingredients without delivering trial-validated single-ingredient doses. Avoid &quot;DL-citrulline&quot; — racemic mixtures are not bioactive equivalents to L-citrulline. The L-form is the biologically active enantiomer. Amazon affiliate link — Prof. Peptide earns a small commission on qualifying purchases at no extra cost to you. We use commissions to fund research and editorial work."
