        "text": "L-citrulline malate is exceptionally well-tolerated at trial doses. The two main practical considerations are mild GI discomfort at single doses above 8 g (split dosing helps) and modest blood pressure reduction (favorable but relevant with antihypertensives). Common (mostly transient) Mild GI discomfort at single doses above 8 g — splitting dose into 2 servings 30 min apart eliminates this. Modest acute blood pressure reduction — generally favorable, relevant with antihypertensive medications. No documented serious adverse events at standard supplemental doses in healthy adults. Less common (watch-list) No herpes outbreak risk. Unlike L-arginine, citrulline doesn&apos;t compete with lysine for cellular transport. No post-MI mortality signal. The VINTAGE-MI safety finding is specific to L-arginine. Citrulline has not shown an analogous adverse signal. Pregnancy and breastfeeding safety isn't well-characterized. Limited trial data — coordinate with obstetrician if pregnant or nursing. Combining with stim-heavy pre-workouts. Citrulline malate is non-stim; if combined with high-caffeine pre-workouts, monitor heart rate and BP during sessions. Drug and supplement interactions Nitrate medications (nitroglycerin, isosorbide). DO NOT COMBINE. Same NO/cGMP convergence as PDE5 inhibitors + nitrates — combination produces dangerous hypotension. PDE5 inhibitors (sildenafil, tadalafil). Additive vasodilation — generally tolerated but coordinate with prescriber. Antihypertensive medications. Additive acute BP-lowering during the pre-workout dosing window. Monitor. Anticoagulants and antiplatelets — modest theoretical bleeding risk via NO-mediated platelet inhibition. Generally tolerated. Other NO-pathway supplements (pure L-citrulline, L-arginine, beetroot) — additive. Pick one citrulline source; beetroot is a separate pathway and can stack."
