        "q": "Glycinate vs citrate vs oxide vs threonate — which magnesium form should I take?",
        "a": "Depends entirely on your goal — this is the central practical decision. Magnesium glycinate (bisglycinate) is gentle on GI, well-absorbed, and the default choice for general supplementation, sleep/anxiety contexts, and users who want magnesium without laxative effect. Magnesium citrate is well-absorbed and produces mild osmotic laxative effect — useful when constipation is part of the goal. Magnesium oxide has poor elemental bioavailability (~4%) but very strong osmotic effect — cheap and effective specifically for constipation, poor for general status correction. Magnesium L-threonate is marketed for cognitive applications based on Liu 2010 rodent data showing brain magnesium increases; human evidence is thin (Liu 2016 small trial) and the form is much more expensive than alternatives. Magnesium malate combines magnesium with malic acid — common in fatigue and fibromyalgia contexts. Practical default: glycinate for general use and sleep; citrate if constipation is also a goal; threonate only if specifically targeting cognitive applications and you accept the thin evidence + cost."
