        "text": "Quercetin has a favorable safety profile at typical supplemental doses. The main practical considerations are mild GI effects, cytochrome P450 drug-metabolism interference (clinically meaningful for some medications), and kidney stress at very high chronic doses. Common (mostly transient) Mild GI discomfort — nausea, stomach upset, especially at higher doses or empty stomach. Resolves with food coadministration. Headache — occasional, generally mild and dose-related. Tingling in extremities — uncommon, dose-related at very high intake. No documented serious adverse events at standard supplemental doses across the human RCT base. Less common (watch-list) Kidney stress at high chronic doses (1,500+ mg/day for months) — modest signal in some studies. Stay within recommended ranges; users with kidney disease should defer to nephrologist. Mild antiplatelet effect — relevant for surgery (1-2 week pre-op washout) and chronic anticoagulant users. Cytochrome P450 inhibition — affects metabolism of multiple medications; clinically meaningful for narrow-therapeutic-index drugs. Drug and supplement interactions Cyclosporine (immunosuppressant). Quercetin inhibits cyclosporine metabolism — can elevate cyclosporine levels and increase toxicity risk. Coordinate with prescribing clinician. Anticoagulants (warfarin, apixaban, rivaroxaban). Mild additive antiplatelet effect. Monitor for unusual bleeding. Quinolone antibiotics (ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin). Quercetin can reduce quinolone effectiveness by competing for DNA gyrase binding. Separate dosing by 2+ hours. CYP3A4 / CYP2C9 substrates. Quercetin inhibits these cytochrome P450 enzymes; may affect metabolism of statins, calcium channel blockers, midazolam, warfarin. Coordinate with clinician for chronic high-dose use. Chemotherapy — quercetin has both pro- and anti-cancer effects depending on context; coordinate with oncologist. Pregnancy and breastfeeding — insufficient supplemental-dose data; defer to clinician. Dietary intake from food is fine."
