        "heading": "How to take vitamin C",
        "text": "Vitamin C is taken orally as tablets, capsules, powder (best for high doses), or chewable forms. The practical considerations are dose splitting (absorption plateau at 200 mg single doses), pairing with iron-containing meals for absorption enhancement, and avoiding immediate-around-training mega-doses for serious athletes. Aspect Recommendation Frequency 1–3× daily depending on dose. For 200–500 mg: 1× daily is fine. For 1000+ mg: split into 2–3 servings (absorption plateaus around 200 mg single dose). For acute illness protocols: every few hours. Best time of day Any time. Morning with breakfast pairs with iron-containing breakfasts. For exercise recovery: post-workout rather than pre-workout. For serious athletes targeting adaptation: outside training window. Food With or without food per tolerance. WITH iron-containing meals (or iron supplements) for the absorption-enhancement effect. Avoid taking with calcium supplements or coffee/tea — those reduce iron absorption. Form Ascorbic acid is the standard. Sodium ascorbate (buffered) for users with sensitive stomach. Liposomal at high doses if absorption is the limiting factor. Powder for cost-efficient high-dose use. Avoid &quot;Ester-C&quot; marketing claims — not well-supported. Standardization marker Pharmaceutical-grade ascorbic acid from cGMP-certified facility. Third-party tested. Reputable brands: Now Foods, Thorne, Pure Encapsulations, Doctor&apos;s Best, Solgar, Life Extension, NutraBio. Cycling / storage No cycling required — chronic daily use is the trial-validated norm. Store in cool dry conditions; vitamin C is degraded by heat, light, and moisture over time. Use opened bottles within 12 months."
