        "heading": "What we don't know yet about vitamin C",
        "text": "Vitamin C is one of the longest-studied supplements but several questions remain — particularly around optimal dose, individual variability, and where the boundaries are between beneficial and counterproductive doses. Optimal supplemental dose for outcomes beyond deficiency prevention. RDA prevents scurvy. Optimal dose for cardiovascular protection, cancer prevention, cognitive aging, and other long-term outcomes is debated. Trials at 200–1000 mg/day are the practical standard but the dose-response above RDA isn&apos;t precisely characterized. Individual responder variability. Why some users see meaningful cold-duration effects and others don&apos;t isn&apos;t fully characterized. Possibly related to baseline vitamin C status, gut microbiome, genetic polymorphisms in vitamin C transporters (SVCT1, SVCT2). Training adaptation blunting dose-response. Paulsen 2014 and similar trials suggest very high antioxidant supplementation may blunt training adaptations. Precise threshold above which this happens — and whether the effect applies across all training modalities — isn&apos;t precisely characterized. IV vitamin C clinical role. Pharmacokinetic distinction from oral is clear (Padayatty 2004); clinical-outcome translation in oncology and sepsis remains mixed. Specific patient subgroups and integration timing may matter more than has been characterized in current trials. Liposomal vs standard oral at therapeutic doses. Higher plasma levels with liposomal at high doses are documented; whether the higher plasma levels translate to better clinical outcomes for specific applications isn&apos;t precisely characterized in head-to-head trials. Cancer prevention vs cancer adjunct. Cancer prevention RCTs (Physicians&apos; Health Study and similar) have been largely null. Cancer adjunct evidence for IV vitamin C is mixed. The biology suggests some role exists but operationalizing it isn&apos;t fully resolved. Aging and vitamin C status. Some research suggests older adults have lower baseline plasma vitamin C despite adequate intake — possibly related to absorption decline. Whether higher supplemental doses are warranted in older adults isn&apos;t precisely characterized. Synergy with other nutrients. Vitamin C&apos;s role in vitamin E recycling, the broader antioxidant network, and collagen synthesis cofactor function suggests synergistic effects with co-nutrient supplementation. Magnitude and clinical translation isn&apos;t precisely characterized for most pairings."
