        "heading": "Page content",
        "text": "Back to Supplement Library Vitamin D3 Recovery &amp; Tissue Repair Last reviewed: May 28, 2026 Also Known As: Cholecalciferol (D3 — preferred supplemental form), ergocalciferol (D2 — less effective), 25-hydroxyvitamin D / calcidiol (storage form measured by blood tests), 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D / calcitriol (active hormonal form), the &quot;sunshine vitamin&quot; Supplement Class: Fat-soluble essential vitamin / hormonal nuclear receptor ligand (VDR — expressed in virtually every cell) / 200+ gene expression regulator / calcium absorption activator / immune modulator / synthesized in skin from UVB exposure Evidence Tier: Sub-tier breakdown (vitamin-C parent pattern). Strong for deficiency correction (rickets/osteomalacia prevention). Strong for bone health WITH calcium in deficient/elderly — Bolland 2018 meta documents combined D + calcium produces bone benefit in deficient elderly specifically (the cleanest endpoint). Modest for respiratory infection prevention — Martineau 2017 BMJ meta of 25 RCTs; larger effect in baseline-deficient users. Moderate/contested for fall reduction — USPSTF 2018 recommends AGAINST routine supplementation for fall prevention; Sanders 2010 JAMA documented annual high-bolus dosing paradoxically INCREASED falls (~15%) and fractures (~26%); older Bischoff-Ferrari meta supported moderate-dose effect. Negative for cancer / CV primary prevention — VITAL trial (Manson 2019 NEJM) null on primary endpoints. Moderate for pregnancy outcomes, MS progression . Mixed for mood, autoimmune, COVID-19 . What is vitamin D3? Vitamin D3 (cholecalciferol) is a fat-soluble essential vitamin that functions more like a hormone than a traditional vitamin — it binds nuclear vitamin D receptors (VDR) expressed in virtually every cell of the body, regulating expression of 200+ genes involved in calcium metabolism, immune function, inflammation, cell growth, and cardiovascular function. It is synthesized in skin from 7-dehydrocholesterol upon UVB exposure, or obtained from diet (fatty fish, egg yolks, fortified foods) and supplementation. The activation cascade: D3 is converted in the liver to 25-hydroxyvitamin D (25-OH-D, calcidiol — the circulating storage form measured by blood tests), then in the kidneys to 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D (1,25-D, calcitriol — the active hormonal form). Vitamin D deficiency is one of the most common nutrient deficiencies globally — affecting approximately 40% of US adults and up to 80% in some populations (northern latitudes, darker skin pigmentation, sun avoidance, indoor lifestyles, advanced age). The evidence picture is best understood through a multi-tier sub-tier breakdown that mirrors the vitamin C parent pattern : Strong evidence for deficiency correction (rickets/osteomalacia prevention) and bone health benefit with calcium in deficient/elderly populations — Bolland 2018 meta documented bone benefit emerges specifically with combined D + calcium in deficient elderly, making this the cleanest Strong endpoint. Modest evidence for respiratory infection prevention — Martineau 2017 BMJ meta of 25 RCTs documented modest acute respiratory tract infection reduction, with larger effect in users baseline-deficient (25-OH-D below 25 nmol/L). Moderate/contested evidence for fall reduction in older adults — USPSTF 2018 recommended AGAINST routine supplementation for fall prevention based on insufficient/inconsistent evidence; Sanders 2010 JAMA documented annual 500,000 IU high-bolus dosing paradoxically INCREASED falls (~15%) and fractures (~26%) compared to placebo — this is the central nuance that reframed the older Bischoff-Ferrari fall-reduction enthusiasm. Negative primary endpoints for cancer and cardiovascular disease prevention — the VITAL trial (Manson 2019 NEJM) randomized 25,871 adults to 2000 IU/day vitamin D3 vs placebo over 5 years and found NO significant reduction in invasive cancer, major cardiovascular events, or total mortality. Subgroup signals (cancer mortality, advanced cancer, autoimmune disease incidence) exist but the primary hypotheses weren&apos;t supported. The honest framing: observational associations between low vitamin D and multiple disease outcomes don&apos;t consistently translate to RCT supplementation benefit in already-replete populations. Supplementation in deficient users to correct deficiency has clear value; supplementation in already-replete users for primary disease prevention has weak support. Form: D3 (cholecalciferol) is approximately 87% more effective than D2 (ergocalciferol) at raising 25-OH-D — always choose D3. Magnesium is required as cofactor for vitamin D activation enzymes (deficient magnesium → impaired vitamin D response — common corrective intervention for non-responders). K2 (MK-7 form) co-supplementation has plausible mechanism (calcium routing to bone vs arteries) but outcome evidence isn&apos;t definitively established. Dosing should be individualized based on baseline 25-OH-D status — test before supplementing for accurate dose selection. Reported benefits: Deficiency prevention (rickets in children; osteomalacia in adults) Bone health with calcium in deficient/elderly populations (Bolland 2018 — cleanest Strong endpoint) Modest acute respiratory tract infection prevention in deficient users (Martineau 2017) Calcium absorption support (30–40% with adequate D vs 10–15% deficient) Immune modulation via VDR signaling on T cells, B cells, macrophages Pregnancy outcome support (reduced preterm birth, pre-eclampsia in deficient mothers) Multiple sclerosis adjunct (reduced relapses at higher doses, neurology supervision) Gut barrier and VDR-mediated intestinal epithelial support Common dose: 1000–2000 IU/day general maintenance (Endocrine Society); 4000–10,000 IU/day for documented deficiency correction (6–12 weeks under provider guidance), then maintenance. Target 25-OH-D: 30–50 ng/mL. Form: D3 (cholecalciferol) — always preferred over D2. Take with fat-containing meal. Test 25-OH-D before starting and at 3 months for individualization. Watch for: Avoid high-bolus dosing for fall prevention (Sanders 2010 paradox); don&apos;t expect cancer or CV primary prevention benefits (VITAL trial null); hypercalcemia at very high chronic doses (10,000+ IU/day without monitoring); sarcoidosis, primary hyperparathyroidism, certain lymphomas (altered vitamin D metabolism — clinician supervision); thiazide diuretic and digoxin interactions (hypercalcemia risk); ensure adequate magnesium status (cofactor for activation — common cause of non-response); obese users often need higher doses (vitamin D distributes into body fat). [[REDACTED]] Related Supplements Magnesium Zinc Vitamin C Fish Oil Probiotics Collagen Peptides"
