        "heading": "Side effects and interactions",
        "text": "Zinc is generally well-tolerated at standard supplemental doses. The main practical considerations are nausea on empty stomach (most common), copper depletion at chronic high doses, lab/medication interactions, and the cold-lozenge metallic taste. Common (mostly transient) Nausea — particularly on empty stomach. Most common reason users discontinue supplementation. Taking with food resolves in most cases. Metallic taste with lozenges — expected from the cold-treatment protocol. Resolves when lozenges stop. No documented serious adverse events at standard supplemental doses (15–30 mg/day) in healthy adults. Less common (watch-list) Copper deficiency from chronic high-dose zinc. Chronic supplementation above 25 mg/day for months can cause copper deficiency — causing anemia, neutropenia, immune suppression, neurological problems, and reduced HDL cholesterol. Mitigation: 1–2 mg copper for chronic doses above 25 mg/day. Reduced HDL cholesterol at chronic high doses. Often actually copper-deficiency-mediated. Resolves with copper co-supplementation. Immune suppression at chronic mega-doses. Doses above 50 mg/day chronically can paradoxically impair immune function (opposite of acute lozenge protocol). Loss of taste/smell with intranasal zinc. Nasal sprays/swabs containing zinc have been associated with anosmia — avoid intranasal zinc products. Oral and lozenge forms are safe. GI distress at high doses. Doses above 50 mg single-dose commonly cause nausea, vomiting, abdominal cramps. Drug and supplement interactions Antibiotics (tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones). Zinc reduces antibiotic absorption by chelation. Separate by 2+ hours. Penicillamine. Zinc reduces penicillamine absorption — relevant for Wilson disease treatment. Thiazide diuretics. May increase urinary zinc excretion; consider supplementation in long-term users. Iron and calcium supplements. Compete for absorption — separate timing. Proton pump inhibitors (PPIs). Long-term PPI use may reduce zinc absorption (acid is needed for solubility). Pregnancy and breastfeeding — supplementation generally safe at standard doses; coordinate with obstetrician for higher doses. Immunosuppressants — theoretical interaction via immune cell modulation. Coordinate with prescribing physician."
