        "heading": "Page content",
        "text": "Back to Supplement Library Zinc-Carnosine Recovery &amp; Tissue Repair Gut Health &amp; Immunity Last reviewed: May 24, 2026 Also Known As: Polaprezinc, zinc L-carnosine, ZnC, PepZin GI (US branded), Promac (Japan Rx brand) Supplement Class: Chelated mineral-dipeptide complex (zinc + L-carnosine, 1:1 molar ratio) Evidence Tier: Strong — 30+ years of Japanese prescription use (polaprezinc/Promac, approved 1994) for gastric ulcer + H. pylori adjunct therapy; Mahmood 2007 (Gut) mechanism RCT in human volunteers; Davison 2016 (Am J Clin Nutr) athlete gut-permeability RCT; Hewlings & Kalman 2020 comprehensive review What is Zinc-Carnosine? Zinc-carnosine (polaprezinc) is a chelated 1:1 molar complex of zinc and the dipeptide L-carnosine. The chelation is what matters: the complex physically adheres to damaged gastrointestinal mucosa rather than being rapidly absorbed, concentrating its protective and healing actions at sites of injury along the GI tract. The compound has been used as a prescription gastric ulcer drug in Japan since 1994 (brand name Promac) and has 30+ years of clinical use data behind it. Western functional medicine adopted it for leaky-gut and NSAID-protection protocols starting in the 2000s, with mechanism-grade RCT support from Mahmood 2007 (Gut journal) and athlete-application evidence from Davison 2016 (Am J Clin Nutr). It is one of the few gut-healing supplements with prescription-grade evidence behind it — and one of the most natural complementary pairings for the gut-healing peptide cluster ( BPC-157 , KPV ). Reported benefits: Gastric ulcer prevention and healing (Japanese Rx evidence base, 30+ years) H. pylori eradication adjunct (improved rates when added to antibiotic triple therapy) Leaky gut / intestinal permeability reduction (Mahmood 2007, Gut journal) NSAID-induced gastric and small-intestinal damage protection Exercise-induced gut permeability reduction in endurance athletes (Davison 2016) Oral mucositis severity reduction (chemotherapy and radiation populations) Antioxidant protection at mucosal surfaces along the full GI tract Common dose: 75 mg twice daily with meals (the Japanese Rx polaprezinc protocol) for general gut-healing and ulcer applications. The Davison 2016 athlete trial used 37.5 mg BID for 14 days pre-event and demonstrated efficacy at the lower dose. For NSAID adjunct use: 75 mg with each NSAID dose. Each 75 mg capsule contains ~17 mg elemental zinc — audit total zinc intake from all sources to stay below the 40 mg/day UL. Where to buy: Available over-the-counter from most supplement retailers. Quality varies — review the quality-markers checklist in the Where to Buy section below before purchasing. [[REDACTED]] Related Supplements L-Glutamine Probiotics Collagen Peptides Zinc Curcumin"
